From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: a notification in the shade is parted by a rule, not boxed

0059 gave every notification row a solid outline, because this SystemUI draws
in software and the elevation shadow AOSP parts rows with is never drawn there.
It works -- the rows have an edge. But four edges per row is a box, and a shade
with three notifications in it read as three boxes stacked in a column. Shown
that screenshot on 2026-10-28, the reader asked for what a list is normally
parted by: one thin line between one notification and the next.

A heads-up notification is not that. It is a surface floating over whatever the
reader was looking at, with the reader's page behind it on three sides, and an
outline is exactly what rule 3 of docs/design/eink-ui.md asks for there. So the
shape now follows what the row is at that moment -- `isPinned()`, which is true
only while the row is floating as a heads-up -- and a pinned row keeps the
outline 0059 drew for it, unchanged. That is also why the `<corners>` 0059 put
in the two background drawables stay: the fill still has to follow that curve
when there is one. In the shade, where the fill is white on a white shade, the
rounded corners it keeps are white on white.

Where the edge is drawn had to change with the shape. 0059 drew it in
`NotificationBackgroundView`, a sibling view behind the row's content, and that
was already visible there: the action row of an expanded notification
(`notification_material_action_list.xml`) is `match_parent` wide, 48dp high and
`layout_gravity="bottom"`, so its background is an opaque band flush with the
bottom edge of the row. Measured on V86, it left not one black pixel at the
row's left edge for the height of the actions. A rule along the bottom edge is
precisely the part of the outline that band covers, so the edge moves to
`ExpandableNotificationRow.dispatchDraw()`, after the children, where nothing
the content paints can reach it. 0060 makes that band white, which is the
colour of the row, but white is paint too: a rule behind it would be gone just
the same.

`ExpandableNotificationRow` and not `ActivatableNotificationView`, which is
where `dispatchDraw` is already overridden: the other subclass of that class is
`NotificationShelf`, and the shelf is a strip of icons, not an entry in a list
and not a surface floating over a page. One thing that existing override does
is not inherited by drawing after it -- the canvas translation of the appear
animation, which moves the children while a row is being added or removed. For
the one frame of it this panel renders (134 to 463 ms each, against an
animation of about 350 ms) the edge stays at the row's own rectangle. Reading
`mAppearAnimationTranslation` out of the parent class would be a field of state
for a line, and the line is in the right place before and after.

Two values 0059 brought with it go back to what AOSP had:

  * `notification_divider_height` went from 0.5dp to 4dp because two outlines
    meeting across a one-pixel gap would read as a single thick line. One rule
    cannot meet itself, so the gap goes back to the one pixel AOSP asks for,
    and the rule plus that pixel is the whole boundary;
  * `notification_children_container_divider_height` was pinned at 0.5dp when
    the value it used to point at stopped being 0.5dp. It can point at it
    again. Between two children of an expanded group that line and the child's
    own rule now land next to each other, 1 px of grey 97 beside 2 px of black:
    a group's inner boundary is a pixel thicker than the boundary between two
    top-level rows, which is the order those two should be in.

What the edge no longer gets is the background's alpha. 0059 had to hand it
over, because `ActivatableNotificationView` fades the normal background of a
row that cannot be dimmed while the shade dims, and a black frame around a fill
that was no longer there would have been worse than no frame. An edge drawn
after the children is not in that fade, and should not be: the dimmed
background takes the normal one's place, so the row always has a fill, and the
boundary between two rows is not a thing that dims. `setDrawableAlpha()` goes
back to the one line AOSP wrote.

The edge is `pw3_outline` at `pw3_outline_width` -- the same black and the same
2 px as every other edge this port draws (1dp at density 1.875, rounded by
`getDimensionPixelSize`). `getActualHeight()` and `getClipBottomAmount()`
rather than `getHeight()`: that pair is the rectangle the background is drawn
between, so the edge ends up where the fill ends, a row collapsing under the
shelf keeps it there, and nothing has to decide whether this row has a fill to
draw an edge for -- if the fill shows, the edge shows. Anti-aliasing stays on
for the sake of the pinned row's corners, and costs the rule nothing: a stroke
of whole-pixel width `w` centred on `bottom - w/2` covers the pixel rows
`bottom - w` to `bottom` exactly, so there is no fractional coverage for it to
soften into the grey rule 1 rules out.

The last row in the shade gets a rule under it with nothing below it. That is a
list closing itself off -- the shade's own footer comes next -- and the
alternative is the stack telling every row whether it is the last one, which is
state, and an answer that changes every time a notification arrives or leaves.

